 Three qualifications bound what a ceiling means in practice.
 
-\paragraph{It is a statement about overlap, not about either retriever.} Two retrievers that both return $K$ documents and share none of them produce the same ceiling as one retriever and one empty list. So does a keyword list of one or two documents that the dense retriever did not return. In the incident, the keyword retriever returned rows on four of the ten replayed queries and the list was at the ceiling on all ten (Table~\ref{tab:prod}): those four keyword lists held one to three chunks, none of them in the dense top 60. The ceiling says no document was reinforced by agreement. The per-retriever \emph{row counts}, the number of documents each retriever returned for the query, say whether that is because a list is empty, nearly empty, or nonempty and disjoint, and a system that fuses should log both.
+\paragraph{It is a statement about overlap, not about either retriever.} Two retrievers that both return $K$ documents and share none of them produce the same ceiling as one retriever and one empty list. So does a keyword list of one to three documents that the dense retriever did not return. In the incident (Section~\ref{sec:incident}), the keyword retriever returned rows on four of the ten replayed queries and the fused list was at the ceiling on all ten: those four keyword lists held one to three chunks, none of them in the dense top 60. The ceiling says no document was reinforced by agreement. The row counts say whether the keyword list was empty, short or full, and a ceiling over a full list means two full lists that share nothing.
 
-\paragraph{The equivalence needs $K \le k$.} One direction holds at any depth: a maximum above $1/k$ proves that some document was returned by two retrievers, because a singleton scores at most $1/k$ for every $K$. The other direction does not. With $K > k$, a document ranked deep in both lists scores below $1/k$ (rank 90 in both at $k = 60$ gives $2/150$), and one ranked exactly $k$ in both scores exactly $1/k$; two ranks $r_1, r_2$ combine to more than $1/k$ exactly when $r_1 r_2 < k^2$. At $K > k$, then, a maximum of $1/k$ shows that every retriever's top document is exclusive to it and leaves deeper overlap open. A system that keeps per-retriever ranks should count shared documents directly. The experiments in Section~\ref{sec:results} run at $K = 100 > k = 60$, report both, and measure how often the two disagree.
+\paragraph{The equivalence needs $K \le k$.} A document at 0-based ranks $r_1$ and $r_2$ in two lists scores above $1/k$ if and only if $r_1 r_2 < k^2$. Ranks 60 and 60 score exactly $1/60$; ranks 90 and 90 score $2/150$. A maximum of $1/k$ therefore rules out a shared document at rank 0 of either list at any depth, and at $K \le k$ it rules out every shared document, because $r_1 r_2 \le (K-1)^2 < k^2$. At $K > k$ it leaves open any shared document whose ranks multiply to at least $k^2$. One direction survives at any depth: a maximum above $1/k$ proves that some document was returned by two retrievers. A system that keeps per-retriever ranks should count shared documents directly. The experiments in Section~\ref{sec:results} run at $K = 100 > k = 60$, report both, and measure how often the two disagree.
 
-\paragraph{With two retrievers, an empty list can never be missed.} If one of two lists is empty and the other is not, every fused document is a singleton and the list is at the ceiling. So with two retrievers the test has no false negatives for an empty list; everything it gets wrong is in the other direction, a ceiling over a nonempty list. With three or more retrievers an empty list can hide behind two nonempty lists that overlap. The equivalence itself holds for any $L$.
+\paragraph{With two retrievers, an empty list cannot be missed.} If one of two lists is empty and the other is not, every fused document is a singleton and the list is at the ceiling. So with two retrievers, as long as the other retriever returned something, the test has no false negatives for an empty list; everything it gets wrong is in the other direction, a ceiling over a nonempty list. With three or more retrievers an empty list can hide behind two nonempty lists that overlap. The equivalence itself holds for any $L$.
 
-\section{Related work}
-\label{sec:related}
