+\section{Related work}
+\label{sec:related}
 
+\citet{cormack2009rrf} proposed reciprocal rank fusion and set $k = 60$ by experiment; the ceiling is arithmetic on their formula, and the system in the incident used their constant unchanged, which is what made a $1/60$ maximum recognisable on sight. The older fusion literature studied the overlap between the lists being fused. CombSUM sums the scores a document receives and CombMNZ multiplies that sum by the number of systems that returned the document \citep{fox1994combination}, so CombMNZ rewards agreement explicitly. \citet{lee1997analyses} showed that fusion helps when the systems agree on relevant documents more than on non-relevant ones; \citet{aslam2001metasearch} modelled metasearch combination, and \citet{montague2001normalization} treated the score-scale problem that rank fusion sidesteps. \citet{nuray2006ranking} went the other way, ranking retrieval systems by their agreement with the fused output and without relevance judgements; the ceiling is a degenerate case of that idea, read at the point where agreement is zero. Rank-biased overlap \citep{webber2010rbo} is the principled measure of agreement between two indefinite rankings; the count of shared documents in Section~\ref{sec:results} is its crudest relative. On hybrid lexical and dense retrieval, \citet{luan2021sparse} and \citet{gao2021clear} establish the complementarity a hybrid relies on, \citet{wang2021interpolation} and \citet{chen2022hybrid} measure what BM25 adds to dense retrievers on BEIR, and \citet{bruch2023fusion} analyse fusion functions and show when RRF is and is not competitive with a tuned convex combination. The ceiling sits upstream of that choice: it is a fact about the scores RRF has already assigned, whatever is in the lists.
+
+That a conjunctive keyword query fails on verbose, natural-language input is an old observation: \citet{kumaran2007shorter} and \citet{bendersky2008verbose} both start from search engines doing badly on long queries and look for the few terms worth keeping, which is what the stopword drop in Section~\ref{sec:found} does crudely. Query performance prediction \citep{cronentownsend2002qpp} asks, per query, whether a retriever did well. The ceiling asks a cruder question of the fusion, whether its inputs agreed on anything, and the logging it calls for is the kind \citet{cooper1998monitoring} argued retrieval systems should carry. \citet{kamphuis2020bm25} show how much BM25 scores vary across implementations of the same formula; the FTS5 variant here is one more, and its query grammar, not its scoring, is what failed. I know of no earlier statement of the $1/k$ maximum as an overlap test, and of no published report of a silently inert retriever inside a hybrid; that is what I could find, not evidence that there is none.
+
 \section{Limitations}
 \label{sec:limits}
 
-The production measurements are on ten and fifteen queries over private corpora and are reported as aggregates the reader cannot check; they establish that the failure happened and how it looked, not an effect size, and the claim that the keyword retriever had been inert since the system's first version is an inference from unchanged code. The public reproduction uses six collections and three encoders of one size class (768-dimensional, base-sized) and FTS5's default BM25 weighting of title and text. The three encoders rerun the same queries, so the 18 pairs are not 18 independent samples, and the paired tests are corrected within each family of 18 but not across the six families; corrected over all 108 tests at once, 23 of the 31 differences significant within their families remain so, among them all seven significant comparisons of the repaired hybrid with the dense retriever at nDCG@10. The collections differ in several ways at once, so the orderings reported here (empty rate against content words per query, short-list disjointness across collections of different size) are observations over five or six points, not controlled effects, and the pooled row-count rates mix the two builders, whose short lists arise differently. The truncation sweep covers only the two smallest collections. The exact ceiling statement needs $K \le k$, and the guarantee that an empty list is never missed needs two retrievers; the common $K = 100$, $k = 60$ setting gets the one-sided direction and the direct overlap count instead, and it produced two ceilings with overlap present. The ceiling detects zero overlap; attributing it to an empty list needs the per-retriever row counts, and a retriever that returns one useless document the other retriever also returns, as on ArguAna, passes both the ceiling and a zero-row check. The negative results in Appendix~\ref{app:negative} are negative on one system each.
